WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.285
Lesson 5.4.

00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:05.913
Smart contracts: state, proxies, admin keys.

00:00:05.913 --> 00:00:08.373
By the end, you'll be able to read exactly

00:00:08.373 --> 00:00:11.234
who controls a contract, and whether its code can

00:00:11.234 --> 00:00:14.438
genuinely change after you've already deposited into it.

00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:17.028
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.028 --> 00:00:20.282
What state, functions, and events actually are, and

00:00:20.282 --> 00:00:23.523
why verified source code isn't proof of safety.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:25.701
Proxies, in full, and exactly who

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:28.563
controls upgrades once you've deposited.

00:00:28.563 --> 00:00:30.409
Admin keys and roles, and the

00:00:30.409 --> 00:00:33.108
specific powers they can actually hold.

00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:35.697
And a full worked example, reading a real

00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:38.718
vault's real setup, directly on a block explorer.

00:00:40.018 --> 00:00:41.728
State, functions, and events.

00:00:43.028 --> 00:00:46.623
Here's what these three actually mean, underneath the jargon.

00:00:46.623 --> 00:00:49.678
State is simply what a contract stores: balances,

00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:53.033
parameters, whatever it actually holds and tracks.

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:55.232
Functions are what change that state,

00:00:55.232 --> 00:00:57.788
the actual actions the contract allows.

00:00:57.788 --> 00:01:00.332
And events are the log, the on-chain record

00:01:00.332 --> 00:01:03.233
of what actually happened, when, and to whom.

00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:05.592
Reading a contract, at its most basic

00:01:05.592 --> 00:01:08.075
level, is reading exactly what state it

00:01:08.075 --> 00:01:10.558
holds, and what functions can change it.

00:01:11.858 --> 00:01:14.879
Worth being completely direct about verified source

00:01:14.879 --> 00:01:17.552
code specifically, since it's easy to mistake

00:01:17.552 --> 00:01:19.363
it for a safety guarantee.

00:01:19.363 --> 00:01:22.326
Verified simply means the code published on the

00:01:22.326 --> 00:01:26.763
block explorer genuinely matches what's actually deployed on-chain.

00:01:26.763 --> 00:01:29.148
That's good, and genuinely worth checking.

00:01:29.148 --> 00:01:31.175
It is not proof the code itself

00:01:31.175 --> 00:01:33.202
is safe, bug-free, or free of a

00:01:33.202 --> 00:01:36.623
deliberately malicious function hiding in plain sight.

00:01:37.923 --> 00:01:40.755
Worth naming why events specifically matter to

00:01:40.755 --> 00:01:43.347
you, beyond just being a technical detail,

00:01:43.347 --> 00:01:46.238
since they're genuinely useful in practice.

00:01:46.238 --> 00:01:48.893
Events are exactly what lets you, or any

00:01:48.893 --> 00:01:52.519
monitoring tool, watch a contract's real activity after

00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:56.275
the fact: every deposit, every withdrawal, every upgrade,

00:01:56.275 --> 00:02:00.419
all logged, timestamped, and publicly readable, without needing

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:03.463
to trust anyone's own summary of what happened.

00:02:04.763 --> 00:02:05.853
Quick check.

00:02:05.853 --> 00:02:07.923
Does verified source code actually

00:02:07.923 --> 00:02:09.343
mean a contract is safe?

00:02:13.763 --> 00:02:16.452
The answer: no. It means the published code

00:02:16.452 --> 00:02:19.508
matches the deployed code, nothing more than that.

00:02:20.808 --> 00:02:22.008
Proxies, in full.

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:26.450
Here's exactly what happens with a proxy contract,

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.284
the word introduced back in this module's own

00:02:29.284 --> 00:02:31.863
Mastery Starter, now covered in full.

00:02:31.863 --> 00:02:34.113
You interact with one fixed address,

00:02:34.113 --> 00:02:36.298
the same one every single time.

00:02:36.298 --> 00:02:39.563
Behind that address, a separate logic contract actually

00:02:39.563 --> 00:02:42.078
defines what happens when you call it.

00:02:42.078 --> 00:02:44.813
And that logic contract can be swapped out for

00:02:44.813 --> 00:02:48.013
a different one entirely, by whoever holds the upgrade

00:02:48.013 --> 00:02:51.388
permission, without your own address ever changing at all.

00:02:52.688 --> 00:02:55.702
Worth being fair about why proxies exist at all,

00:02:55.702 --> 00:02:59.693
before treating the pattern itself as inherently suspicious.

00:02:59.693 --> 00:03:01.795
They let a team genuinely fix a

00:03:01.795 --> 00:03:04.226
real bug, or add a real improvement,

00:03:04.226 --> 00:03:07.248
without forcing every single user to manually

00:03:07.248 --> 00:03:10.438
migrate to an entirely new contract address.

00:03:10.438 --> 00:03:13.243
That's a legitimate, valuable capability.

00:03:13.243 --> 00:03:16.441
The actual question was never whether a contract is

00:03:16.441 --> 00:03:20.253
a proxy; it's specifically who controls that proxy's upgrade,

00:03:20.253 --> 00:03:22.713
and how much warning you'd actually get.

00:03:24.013 --> 00:03:27.477
Worth being completely precise about the actual consequence

00:03:27.477 --> 00:03:30.028
of this, since it's easy to gloss over.

00:03:30.028 --> 00:03:34.459
Whoever controls a proxy's upgrade permission genuinely controls the

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:38.183
rules your deposited funds actually operate under, at any

00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:42.165
future point they choose to exercise it, entirely independent

00:03:42.165 --> 00:03:45.504
of whatever the contract's rules happened to say on

00:03:45.504 --> 00:03:47.623
the day you originally deposited.

00:03:48.923 --> 00:03:50.418
Admin keys, and roles.

00:03:51.718 --> 00:03:54.496
Here's what to actually check about the specific

00:03:54.496 --> 00:03:57.063
admin roles behind any contract you use.

00:03:57.063 --> 00:03:59.551
Owners typically hold the broadest powers,

00:03:59.551 --> 00:04:02.513
often including the upgrade permission itself.

00:04:02.513 --> 00:04:06.772
Guardians usually hold narrower, specifically emergency powers, like

00:04:06.772 --> 00:04:09.973
pausing the contract during an active incident.

00:04:09.973 --> 00:04:12.384
And whether any of these roles sits

00:04:12.384 --> 00:04:16.401
behind a multisig, genuinely requiring multiple independent

00:04:16.401 --> 00:04:18.812
people to agree, or behind a single

00:04:18.812 --> 00:04:21.758
key alone, changes the real risk enormously.

00:04:23.058 --> 00:04:26.100
Here's that exact difference, made concrete, between

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.753
the two ends of the admin-setup spectrum.

00:04:28.753 --> 00:04:33.569
A well-structured multisig, needing several independent signers to agree,

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:37.084
means no single compromised or malicious key can move

00:04:37.084 --> 00:04:40.443
funds, upgrade the contract, or pause it alone.

00:04:40.443 --> 00:04:43.640
A single key holding those same powers means

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:47.051
exactly one compromise, one mistake, or one bad

00:04:47.051 --> 00:04:49.253
actor is all it actually takes.

00:04:50.553 --> 00:04:52.627
Here's exactly how to find all of

00:04:52.627 --> 00:04:55.128
this yourself, on a real block explorer,

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:57.873
completing the first safe step this module's

00:04:57.873 --> 00:05:00.308
own Mastery Starter set up for you.

00:05:00.308 --> 00:05:03.881
Open the contract's page directly, and check its Contract

00:05:03.881 --> 00:05:06.768
tab for a label reading Proxy, or similar.

00:05:06.768 --> 00:05:09.268
Find the actual admin address it names,

00:05:09.268 --> 00:05:11.706
and look up what that specific address

00:05:11.706 --> 00:05:14.193
is, a multisig, or a single wallet.

00:05:14.193 --> 00:05:17.193
And check that address's own transaction history

00:05:17.193 --> 00:05:19.703
for anything resembling a timelock delay.

00:05:21.003 --> 00:05:22.093
Quick check.

00:05:22.093 --> 00:05:23.869
What's the actual red flag to

00:05:23.869 --> 00:05:25.468
look for in an admin setup?

00:05:29.888 --> 00:05:32.711
The answer: a single key that can upgrade the

00:05:32.711 --> 00:05:36.578
contract, or move funds, instantly, with nothing else required.

00:05:37.878 --> 00:05:38.883
Worked example.

00:05:40.183 --> 00:05:43.175
Here's exactly what you'd find, reading a real

00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:46.357
vault's real setup, directly on a block explorer,

00:05:46.357 --> 00:05:49.458
exactly as the source material describes it.

00:05:49.458 --> 00:05:53.023
The contract itself shows, explicitly, as a proxy.

00:05:53.023 --> 00:05:57.061
Its upgrade admin is a three-of-five multisig, meaning three

00:05:57.061 --> 00:06:00.803
of five independent signers genuinely have to agree.

00:06:00.803 --> 00:06:03.766
And that multisig sits behind a forty-eight

00:06:03.766 --> 00:06:06.863
hour timelock, a real, mandatory delay before

00:06:06.863 --> 00:06:09.893
any approved change can actually take effect.

00:06:11.193 --> 00:06:14.834
Here's exactly what that combination actually protects you from,

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:18.158
and precisely how much warning it genuinely gives you.

00:06:18.158 --> 00:06:21.001
No single compromised key can instantly change

00:06:21.001 --> 00:06:23.361
your funds' rules, since three of five

00:06:23.361 --> 00:06:26.323
independent signers would all have to agree.

00:06:26.323 --> 00:06:29.178
And even after they do agree, you'd still have

00:06:29.178 --> 00:06:32.155
a real, full forty-eight hours to notice, and to

00:06:32.155 --> 00:06:35.557
exit, before that approved change actually takes effect.

00:06:36.857 --> 00:06:39.062
The full spectrum, not just two points.

00:06:40.362 --> 00:06:44.085
Here's that same idea, stretched across a wider, purely

00:06:44.085 --> 00:06:48.339
illustrative spectrum, from weakest to strongest, so the actual

00:06:48.339 --> 00:06:52.029
pattern is visible, not just two isolated examples.

00:06:52.029 --> 00:06:54.195
A one-of-one key, with no timelock,

00:06:54.195 --> 00:06:55.939
sits at the weakest end.

00:06:55.939 --> 00:06:59.454
The source material's own three-of-five, with a forty-eight

00:06:59.454 --> 00:07:02.214
hour timelock, sits solidly in the middle.

00:07:02.214 --> 00:07:05.272
And a broader, five-of-nine multisig, behind a

00:07:05.272 --> 00:07:08.656
full seven-day timelock, sits toward the strongest,

00:07:08.656 --> 00:07:11.324
slowest-moving end of that same spectrum.

00:07:12.624 --> 00:07:15.879
Here's the exact same vault, but with a meaningfully

00:07:15.879 --> 00:07:19.679
worse admin setup instead, to make the contrast concrete.

00:07:19.679 --> 00:07:22.664
A one-of-one admin, a single key alone, with no

00:07:22.664 --> 00:07:25.588
timelock at all, means that one key can change

00:07:25.588 --> 00:07:29.133
your funds' rules instantly, with zero warning, and zero

00:07:29.133 --> 00:07:32.429
chance for you to react before it's already happened.

00:07:33.729 --> 00:07:34.819
Quick check.

00:07:34.819 --> 00:07:36.828
Why does a timelock actually matter

00:07:36.828 --> 00:07:38.614
to you, specifically, as a user?

00:07:43.034 --> 00:07:45.349
The answer: it gives you real time to

00:07:45.349 --> 00:07:47.968
actually see a queued change, and to exit,

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:50.709
before that change is allowed to take effect.

00:07:52.009 --> 00:07:53.469
Here's your checklist.

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:54.961
Do it for every contract

00:07:54.961 --> 00:07:57.289
holding meaningful funds of yours.

00:07:57.289 --> 00:07:58.999
Check whether it's a proxy, and

00:07:58.999 --> 00:08:01.489
specifically who can actually upgrade it.

00:08:01.489 --> 00:08:03.982
Know the admin roles, and the multisig

00:08:03.982 --> 00:08:07.051
threshold behind them, precisely, not just that

00:08:07.051 --> 00:08:08.949
a multisig exists at all.

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:11.549
And know the timelock's actual length, and

00:08:11.549 --> 00:08:13.545
set an alert for yourself on any

00:08:13.545 --> 00:08:16.266
queued change, for genuinely large positions.

00:08:17.566 --> 00:08:18.811
Let's recap.

00:08:18.811 --> 00:08:20.747
State is what a contract stores,

00:08:20.747 --> 00:08:23.101
functions change it, events log it.

00:08:23.101 --> 00:08:26.208
Verified source code just means the published code

00:08:26.208 --> 00:08:29.371
matches what's deployed, never proof of safety.

00:08:29.371 --> 00:08:31.917
A proxy's real address stays fixed, but

00:08:31.917 --> 00:08:34.144
the logic behind it can be swapped

00:08:34.144 --> 00:08:36.481
by whoever controls the upgrade.

00:08:36.481 --> 00:08:39.401
A multisig needs several signers to agree;

00:08:39.401 --> 00:08:42.349
a single key needs just one compromise.

00:08:42.349 --> 00:08:45.360
And a timelock is your genuine window to notice,

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.494
and to exit, before a change actually takes effect.

00:08:49.794 --> 00:08:53.120
Worth connecting this back to the dependency map from

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.499
this module's own Mastery Starter, one final time.

00:08:56.499 --> 00:08:59.313
The contract itself, its proxy status, its

00:08:59.313 --> 00:09:01.734
admin, and its timelock, is the last

00:09:01.734 --> 00:09:04.194
specific link in that same chain.

00:09:04.194 --> 00:09:06.565
You now know exactly what to check on

00:09:06.565 --> 00:09:09.373
every single one: the chain, the bridge, the

00:09:09.373 --> 00:09:12.119
L2, the oracle, and now the contract itself.

00:09:13.419 --> 00:09:16.253
Check whether it's a proxy, know the admin roles

00:09:16.253 --> 00:09:19.619
and multisig threshold, and know the timelock length.

00:09:19.619 --> 00:09:22.810
Next up, Lesson 5.5: smart-contract risk

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:24.834
& what audits don't prove.
